030,6078,448,518,3245,825,1008,1697,13,3555,374,279,8028,3204,1372,315,1424,927,2050,30,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\n23 people attend a party. Each person shakes hands with at least one other person. What is the minimum possible number of handshakes?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"9338d52fc261109439117fccf398554aa3f462a3ddafc2a70382a8c500923730"},"seconds":18.684053659439087,"seed":557286055730596303,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem step by step, we need to determine the minimum number of handshakes that can occur when 23 people attend a party and each person shakes hands with at least one other person.\n\n1. **Understanding the Problem**:\n   - There are 23 people.\n   - Each person must shake hands with at least one other person.\n   - We want to minimize the number of handshakes.\n\n2. **Graph Theory Interpretation**:\n   - We can model this problem using graph theory where each person is a vertex and each handshake is an edge.\n   - We need to find a graph with 23 vertices where each vertex has at least one edge (handshake).\n\n3. **Constructing the Minimum Graph**:\n   - The simplest way to ensure each person shakes hands with at least one other person is to form a graph where each person shakes hands with exactly one other person.\n   - This can be done by forming pairs of people who shake hands with each other. Each pair will contribute exactly one handshake.\n\n4. **Counting the Handshakes**:\n   - With 23 people, we can form 11 pairs (since 23 is an odd number, one person will be left out of complete pairing).\n   - Each of these 11 pairs will contribute one handshake.\n   - Thus, there will be 11 handshakes.\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible number of handshakes is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{11}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{11}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
